> Note that the effects might not be obvious, but to anyone reading the
> spoilers, they'll be *immediately* obvious. The smallest possible
> advantage will be used to perfection.

Yep, I know.  The idea behind the subtleness of the effect is to
provide a *slight* advantage, not make it unknowable.  Something like
a +1 to a single type of check isn't something that people will be
clamoring for, but might take advantage of if the chance presents
itself.


> > Finally, having more than one of a gemstone out
> > carries no additional benefit.
> 
> What would be the logic behind that? I can understand it for obvious
> balance reasons, but how would you explain it? Would a luckstone still
> work?

Luckstones would be completely unchanged.  It was late when I wrote
this so I should probably explain, no more than one of each type of
gemstone would work at a time.  So having a ruby and a sapphire out
would each confer their slight benefits, but having additional rubies
and sapphires beyond the first would not be useful.


> And what would prevent me from taking out the one I currently need? This
> does sound a lot like AD&D ioun stones, by the way (of which you can only
> use two at the same time).

Ioun stones are too strong compared to the minor effects I suggest.


> > The second problem with this: gemstones are rare, but light, and once
> > found it's easy to keep a bunch out and soak up the benefits.
> 
> Gemstones aren't rare at all! It's just too much of a hassle to use that
> potion of object detection and start digging away.

But an *individual* gemstone that you might want, like
specifically-topaz, they're typically hard to find in the early-to-mid
game.   Once you reach the end of the Mines there are some guarenteed
gemstones.  Ludios has a good number but if the player can clear out
that place they probably won't need them.

So there's a slim chance of finding the ones you want in the time that
it matters -- not that it would ever matter, greatly.


> Giving mundane items like gemstones magical abilities will just make the
> game more tedious, since almost everyone will start digging away to get
> those buried gems out.

Except that the projected usefulness of gems would be quite
insignificant.

As for tedious: Nethack is already very tedious.  Nethack isn't at all
tedious.  It could be described both ways, and at different stages in
my Nethack-playing career I might have described it as both.  Is it at
a magic point right now where it's just tedious enough?


> All in all, a fun idea, but not very balanced, I guess. There's enough
> magic in the game already, in my opinion.

Hmmm... I'm quite wary of that idea.  I don't think it's ever a good
idea to say "the game's rich enough as it is, we're done in that
area."  Otherwise, why are there so many YANIs these days?  Nethack is
the computer game with the richest game world, hands down, but I don't
think that should be taken as an indication of whether it's rich
enough.

Anyway, I actually the idea would have rather little effect on
gameplay (we're talking about seriously minor effects here), and that
could actually be seen as a big problem with it as I've written it.


